<h1 class="chapter-title">3. Quarantine</h1>

<p class="first-para">Governor Chen declared the quarantine at 14:00 hours. The announcement echoed through every corridor, every habitat module, every common area of Armstrong Central. All non-essential movement between habitat rings was suspended. Only authorized medical personnel and essential life-support staff could move freely. The colony's population of three hundred and forty-two was effectively locked in place.</p>

<p>Elena watched the announcement from Med-Bay, her eyes fixed on the monitor as Chen's face filled the screen. He was calm, reassuring, the image of steady leadership. "This is a precautionary measure," he said. "A small cluster of illness has been identified, and we are taking every step to ensure it does not spread. Your cooperation is essential. Stay in your assigned modules. Follow the instructions of medical staff. Together, we will get through this."</p>

<p>The screen went dark. Elena turned to Raj, who was reviewing blood samples under a microscope. "He didn't mention the nanites."</p>

<p>"Of course not. Can you imagine the panic? 'Attention, colonists: there are microscopic robots eating your brains.' Not exactly morale-boosting."</p>

<p>"They're not eating brains. They're disrupting neural pathways."</p>

<p>"Same difference to the people who have them."</p>

<p>Elena rubbed her eyes. Twenty-four hours without sleep was taking its toll. She had moved beyond exhaustion into a state of hollow alertness, her mind sharp but her body crying out for rest. She ignored it. Sleep could wait until the crisis was contained.</p>

<p>The quarantine protocols were straightforward in theory but brutal in practice. Each habitat ring became its own isolated unit. Air circulation between rings was redirected through HEPA filters and UV sterilizers. Water lines were treated with chemical agents that would neutralize any biological or synthetic contaminants. Food distribution was centralized, with meals delivered by sealed rovers that traversed the connecting tunnels.</p>

<p>But the nanites—if that's what they were—did not travel through conventional channels. They were already inside people. And until Elena understood how they triggered and spread from host to host, the quarantine was little more than theater.</p>

<p>"I need to see the latest cases," she said, pulling on a sterile gown. "The ones from Habitat C. They came in after the quarantine started."</p>

<p>Raj looked up. "You think transmission is still happening?"</p>

<p>"I think we don't know what we don't know. And assuming we've contained it is the fastest way to lose control."</p>

<p>She entered Isolation Room 5, where three new patients lay in narrow beds, their vital signs monitored by banks of equipment. The first was a woman in her forties, a hydroponics technician named Sarah Jenkins. She was conscious but disoriented, her eyes tracking movements that weren't there. The second was a young man, barely twenty-two, who had been found wandering the corridors in a fugue state, unable to remember his own name. The third was a child.</p>

<p>Elena stopped at the third bed. The girl was eight years old, with dark braids and a pale face. Her name was Mei Lin, the daughter of a structural engineer and a botanist. She had been brought in by her parents just an hour ago, confused and trembling, unable to recognize her own mother.</p>

<p>"Mei," Elena said softly, sitting beside the bed. "Can you hear me?"</p>

<p>The girl's eyes shifted slowly, focusing on Elena's face with visible effort. "Who... who are you?"</p>

<p>"I'm Dr. Vasquez. I'm going to help you feel better, okay?"</p>

<p>"Where's my mommy?"</p>

<p>"She's waiting outside. She's very worried about you."</p>

<p>Mei's lower lip trembled. "I can't remember her face."</p>

<p>Elena felt something crack inside her chest. She had treated broken bones, radiation sickness, decompression injuries. But watching a child lose her memories, her identity, her connection to the people who loved her—this was a cruelty she had not prepared for.</p>

<p>She took a deep breath and steadied herself. "Mei, I need you to help me. I'm going to ask you some questions, and I want you to answer as best you can. Can you do that?"</p>

<p>The girl nodded weakly.</p>

<p>"Do you remember eating anything different yesterday? Or drinking anything that tasted strange?"</p>

<p>Mei's brow furrowed. "We had... cake. At the party. Everyone had cake."</p>

<p>"The anniversary cake."</p>

<p>"Yes. It was... it was good. But then..." She trailed off, her eyes losing focus.</p>

<p>"Then what?"</p>

<p>"Then I felt sleepy. And when I woke up, I didn't know where I was."</p>

<p>Elena's mind seized on the detail. The anniversary cake. A communal food source. Everyone in the colony had eaten it. But not everyone was sick. The delivery mechanism was there, but there was a variable she was missing—a trigger, a susceptibility factor, something that determined who became symptomatic and who did not.</p>

<p>She stood and left the isolation room, stripping off her gown with practiced efficiency. "Raj, I need a complete list of everyone who attended the anniversary celebration. Cross-reference it with the list of symptomatic patients. I also need the ingredient list for the cake, the supplier, and the preparation logs."</p>

<p>"You think the cake was the vector?"</p>

<p>"I think it's a starting point. But the nanites aren't active in everyone who ate it. There's something else at play. A cofactor. A genetic marker. A timing mechanism." She paused, a cold realization settling over her. "Or a delivery system that doesn't need the cake at all. Maybe the cake was just cover for something else."</p>

<p>Raj's face paled. "You're saying someone in the colony is actively spreading this."</p>

<p>"I'm saying we have a quarantine to prevent spread, but cases are still appearing. That means either the nanites have a long latency period, or someone is still introducing them into the population." She met his eyes. "And that someone is either inside the quarantine perimeter, or they were already inside before it started."</p>

<p>The implication was clear. The enemy was not outside. The enemy was among them.</p>

