<h1 class="chapter-title">4. The Investigation</h1>

<p class="first-para">Elena assembled an investigation team from the colony's small pool of available expertise. Raj Patel led biological analysis. Dr. Yuki Tanaka, a materials scientist with a background in nanotechnology, took responsibility for understanding the nanites' structure and function. James Kowalski, a former intelligence officer who had retired to the Moon seeking peace, was brought in to handle the forensic and security dimensions of the case. And Elena herself would coordinate, analyze, and pursue the medical thread.</p>

<p>They met in a conference room adjacent to Med-Bay, the walls lined with displays showing patient data, supply chain logs, and environmental monitoring reports. The atmosphere was tense, the weight of the crisis pressing down on each of them.</p>

<p>Kowalski was the first to speak. He was a large man with a weathered face and eyes that missed nothing. "I've been reviewing the security footage from the three days leading up to the anniversary. There are gaps. Seventeen minutes of footage missing from the kitchen prep area on the morning of the celebration. The logs show a power fluctuation, but I've checked the electrical systems. There was no fluctuation."</p>

<p>"Someone deleted the footage," Yuki said. It was not a question.</p>

<p>"Someone with system access. Someone who knew the camera positions and the recording protocols." Kowalski slid a tablet across the table. "I've identified five people with the necessary clearance. Three are in the command structure. One is in IT. One is in life-support management."</p>

<p>Elena studied the list. Governor Chen's name was at the top, followed by Patricia Okonkwo, then the head of security, a man named Dmitri Volkov. The IT specialist was a woman named Anya Sharma. The life-support manager was Thomas Park.</p>

<p>"Any of them showing symptoms?" Elena asked.</p>

<p>"Not yet. But that doesn't mean they're not involved."</p>

<p>"We can't accuse the governor of spreading a biological agent based on seventeen missing minutes of footage," Raj said. "We need more evidence."</p>

<p>"Agreed," Elena said. "Yuki, what have you found on the nanites?"</p>

<p>Yuki pulled up a holographic display, rotating the crystalline structure they had imaged earlier. "I've reverse-engineered the assembly protocol. These nanites are sophisticated. They're not just random machines—they have a programmed objective. They bind to specific neural receptors and disrupt synaptic firing patterns. The effect is memory loss, cognitive decline, and eventually, if left unchecked, complete neural degradation."</p>

<p>"Can they be stopped?"</p>

<p>"Theoretically. They have a power source—a microscopic battery that degrades over approximately seventy-two hours. After that, the nanites become inert and are filtered out by the body. But the damage they do in those seventy-two hours is cumulative and largely irreversible."</p>

<p>"So if we can keep patients alive for three days, the nanites die off naturally," Raj said.</p>

<p>"Yes, but the neurological damage is already done. By day two, most patients have lost significant memory function. By day three, they're in a persistent vegetative state. The nanites stop, but the brain doesn't recover."</p>

<p>Elena felt the weight of the timeline pressing down. "We need an intervention. Something that neutralizes the nanites faster. Or protects neural tissue from damage."</p>

<p>"I'm working on it," Yuki said. "But I need more samples, more data, and more time."</p>

<p>"Time is the one thing we don't have."</p>

<p>Kowalski cleared his throat. "There's another angle. If the nanites were introduced through the food supply, the delivery mechanism had to be prepared in advance. That means someone in the kitchen staff was either involved or compromised. I've interviewed all eight kitchen personnel. One of them—a sous-chef named Diego Reyes—was unusually nervous. His answers were rehearsed. He kept looking at the door."</p>

<p>"Did you detain him?"</p>

<p>"I had him moved to a secure location for further questioning. But I don't have the authority to hold him indefinitely without cause."</p>

<p>"Then find cause. Check his quarters. Check his comm logs. Check his financial transactions." Elena's voice hardened. "We're past the point of civil liberties. People are dying."</p>

<p>Kowalski nodded. "I'll have a full report in four hours."</p>

<p>The meeting adjourned, and the team dispersed to their respective tasks. Elena remained in the conference room, staring at the holographic nanite structure that rotated slowly in the air before her. It was beautiful in a terrible way—a perfect geometric construction, designed with precision and purpose. The work of a skilled engineer. Someone with resources, expertise, and a motive she could not yet see.</p>

<p>Her comm unit buzzed. "Dr. Vasquez, this is Martinez. We have two more cases. Both from Habitat B. Both showing early-stage symptoms."</p>

<p>"Same presentation?"</p>

<p>"Identical. But there's something different. Both of them were part of the team that prepared the anniversary food supplies. Diego Reyes's team."</p>

<p>Elena's pulse quickened. "Are they conscious?"</p>

<p>"Barely. One of them is trying to speak. She keeps saying the same word over and over."</p>

<p>"What word?"</p>

<p>"'Protocol.' She says 'protocol' and then 'Dorado.' Does that mean anything to you?"</p>

<p>Elena's blood ran cold. Dorado. The golden one. In corporate terminology, it was a project code name. She had heard it once before, years ago, during a classified briefing about corporate interests in lunar mining. Dorado was the code name for a consortium that had bid on lunar extraction rights and lost.</p>

<p>"Keep her stable," Elena said. "I'm on my way."</p>

<p>The pieces were beginning to fit together. A corporate consortium, denied access to lunar resources, turning to sabotage. A engineered pathogen delivered through the food supply. A mole inside the colony. And a deadline measured in hours before the colony's population was irreparably damaged.</p>

<p>But knowing the shape of the conspiracy was not the same as stopping it. And Elena knew that the deeper she dug, the more dangerous the truth would become.</p>

