<h1 class="chapter-title">5. Denial</h1>

<p class="first-para">Governor Chen called a closed session of the colony's leadership council at 19:00 hours. Elena attended along with Raj, Yuki, and Kowalski. The council chamber was small, designed for meetings of no more than twelve, its circular table polished to a mirror finish. The faces around that table reflected a spectrum of emotions: fear, suspicion, denial, and—in Chen's case—a steely determination to control the narrative at all costs.</p>

<p>"Let me be clear," Chen said, his voice carrying an edge that Elena had not heard before. "The colony is in a fragile state. Our relationship with Earth's funding bodies is tenuous. If word gets out that we are facing a biological crisis—regardless of origin—the repercussions will be catastrophic. Not just for this colony, but for the entire lunar settlement program."</p>

<p>Patricia Okonkwo nodded in agreement. "We have enemies on Earth who would love to see this project fail. National governments who resent the international cooperation model. Corporate interests who want to privatize lunar resources. If they catch wind of a vulnerability, they will exploit it mercilessly."</p>

<p>"So we lie," Elena said flatly. "We pretend everything is fine while people lose their minds."</p>

<p>"We manage the message," Chen corrected. "There is a difference."</p>

<p>"Is there? Because from where I'm sitting, the difference is invisible. Twelve patients in comas. Four more showing early symptoms. And you want to pretend this is a routine medical incident."</p>

<p>Chen's eyes hardened. "I want to stabilize the colony while you find a cure. Panic will not help anyone. Panic will make things worse."</p>

<p>"What will make things worse is hiding the truth. If this is a deliberate attack—which the evidence strongly suggests—then we have an enemy who knows exactly what they're doing. They're counting on us to cover it up. They're counting on us to be afraid of the political consequences."</p>

<p>The room fell silent. Dmitri Volkov, the head of security, leaned forward. He was a broad-shouldered man with a shaved head and a voice like gravel. "Dr. Vasquez, do you have evidence of who perpetrated this attack?"</p>

<p>"Not yet. But I have a lead. The name Dorado was mentioned by one of the victims. It's a corporate code name. I need access to the colony's classified communications archives to trace it."</p>

<p>"Classified archives are above my clearance level," Volkov said. "And above yours."</p>

<p>"Then Governor Chen can authorize it."</p>

<p>Chen's expression was unreadable. "The classified archives contain sensitive information about Earth-side negotiations, defense protocols, and intelligence assets. I cannot simply open them to a medical investigation."</p>

<p>"People are dying, Governor."</p>

<p>"And I am trying to keep the rest of them alive by maintaining the political support that keeps this colony operational. If you want to find answers, find them within the boundaries I have set. I will not compromise the security of this mission based on a dying woman's ravings."</p>

<p>Elena felt her temper flare, but she forced herself to breathe. Losing control would accomplish nothing. "Then give me something else. Give me access to the supply chain records. Give me the personnel files of everyone who works in food preparation. Give me the communication logs of the senior staff for the past week."</p>

<p>Chen considered the request. "The supply chain records and personnel files are yours. Communication logs of senior staff are off-limits without cause."</p>

<p>"I have cause. Seventeen minutes of missing security footage from the kitchen."</p>

<p>Chen's eyes flickered, a micro-expression that Elena caught and filed away. "I will authorize a review of the footage logs. But I will not authorize surveillance of senior staff. They are the people who keep this colony running. I will not create an atmosphere of suspicion."</p>

<p>"The atmosphere is already suspicious, Governor. Someone in this colony is responsible for the attack. The evidence points to someone with high-level access."</p>

<p>"Then find them without trampling on the rights of innocent people."</p>

<p>The meeting ended shortly after, with no clear resolution. As the council members filed out, Patricia Okonkwo lingered, her eyes meeting Elena's across the table. There was something in her gaze—a warning, perhaps, or a plea. But she said nothing, turning and following Chen out the door.</p>

<p>Raj approached Elena, his voice low. "He's not going to help us. He's too worried about the political fallout."</p>

<p>"Then we work around him."</p>

<p>"That's dangerous. If he finds out we're going behind his back—"</p>

<p>"Then he doesn't find out. Yuki, how close are you to a treatment?"</p>

<p>Yuki looked up from her tablet. "I've identified a compound that might neutralize the nanites. It's a electromagnetic pulse at a specific frequency—enough to disrupt the nanites' internal circuitry without damaging human tissue. But I need to build a delivery device, and I need to test it."</p>

<p>"Build it. Test it on the comatose patients. If it works, we scale up."</p>

<p>"If it doesn't work, we could cause more damage."</p>

<p>Elena met her eyes. "Then make sure it works."</p>

<p>Yuki nodded and left, her steps quick and purposeful. Kowalski remained, his arms crossed. "I'll keep digging into Diego Reyes. But I need to warn you—if the chain of evidence leads to the governor's office, we're going to have a problem."</p>

<p>"I know."</p>

<p>"And if it leads to Earth, we're going to have a bigger problem."</p>

<p>"I know that too."</p>

<p>Kowalski studied her for a moment. "You're not afraid of any of this, are you?"</p>

<p>Elena shook her head. "I'm terrified. But being terrified doesn't mean I get to stop."</p>

<p>She left the council chamber and walked back toward Med-Bay, her mind already racing ahead to the next steps. The denial from leadership was a wall she would have to go around, not through. But there was another path—one that led through the colony's underground, through the people who had been here long enough to know where the secrets were buried.</p>

<p>She needed to find someone who had been on the Moon before the crisis. Someone who knew the old guard, the original settlers, the people who had built this colony from nothing. Someone who remembered the deals that had been made to get them here.</p>

<p>She needed to find the truth that the leadership was so desperate to hide.</p>

