<h1 class="chapter-title">6. The Cover-Up</h1>

<p class="first-para">Elena found Clara Morrison in the botanical gardens, the only place in the colony where natural light was simulated with enough fidelity to grow terrestrial plants. Clara was seventy-three years old, one of the original twelve settlers who had established Armstrong Central a decade ago. She had seen every triumph and every failure, and she had outlived most of her contemporaries. Now she spent her days tending to tomato plants and speaking rarely of the past.</p>

<p>"Clara," Elena said, approaching the old woman. "I need your help."</p>

<p>Clara did not look up from the soil she was turning. "Everyone needs something, Elena. The question is whether I want to give it."</p>

<p>"There's an attack on the colony. A biological agent. People are dying. And the leadership is covering it up."</p>

<p>Clara's hands stopped moving. She straightened slowly, her joints creaking, and fixed Elena with eyes that had seen too much. "What kind of attack?"</p>

<p>"Nanites. Engineered machines that destroy neural tissue. They were introduced through the food supply during the anniversary celebration."</p>

<p>"And Chen is hiding it."</p>

<p>"Yes."</p>

<p>Clara was silent for a long moment, her gaze drifting to the artificial sky above the gardens. "I knew something like this would happen. The moment we became valuable, we became a target."</p>

<p>"Valuable how?"</p>

<p>"The helium-3 deposits in the southern highlands. The richest concentration ever found. A clean energy source that could power Earth for centuries. Every corporation on Earth wants it. And we—the colony—control access."</p>

<p>Elena's mind raced. "The Dorado consortium."</p>

<p>Clara's eyes snapped to hers. "Where did you hear that name?"</p>

<p>"From a patient. Before she slipped into a coma."</p>

<p>"Dorado was the code name for the consortium that lost the mining rights bid five years ago. They were furious. They had spent billions on lobbying, on infrastructure planning, on bribes. When the international panel awarded the rights to the colony instead, Dorado's leadership vowed revenge."</p>

<p>"Why didn't anyone tell me this?"</p>

<p>"Because it's classified. Because the colony's leadership was complicit in the bid process. Because Marcus Chen was the one who negotiated the deal that cut Dorado out."</p>

<p>Elena felt the ground shift beneath her. "Chen was involved in the bid process?"</p>

<p>"He was the lead negotiator for the colony. He knew that denying Dorado would make enemies. He just didn't expect them to strike here, on the Moon."</p>

<p>"Or maybe he did expect it. Maybe that's why he's so keen to cover it up. If the truth comes out, his legacy is destroyed."</p>

<p>Clara nodded slowly. "You're starting to understand. But you don't know the whole story yet."</p>

<p>"Tell me."</p>

<p>"Dorado didn't just lose the bid. They were investigated for bribery and corruption. The investigation was led by... a woman named Patricia Okonkwo. She was a prosecutor for the International Lunar Commission before she became Chen's chief of staff. She built the case against Dorado. She put their executives in prison."</p>

<p>Elena stared. "Patricia Okonkwo was the prosecutor who took down Dorado?"</p>

<p>"She was the best in the business. And Dorado has never forgotten. They swore they would destroy everything she had built."</p>

<p>"Including the colony."</p>

<p>"Including the colony. Including Patricia. Including anyone who stood in their way."</p>

<p>Elena's mind raced through the implications. Patricia Okonkwo was not just Chen's chief of staff—she was a target. And if Dorado had infiltrated the colony, they would want to hurt her in the most devastating way possible. By destroying the community she had helped build. By making her watch as her life's work crumbled.</p>

<p>"I need evidence," Elena said. "Something concrete that links Dorado to the attack."</p>

<p>Clara reached into her pocket and pulled out a data chip, worn and scratched with age. "This is the original investigation file. Patricia's work. She gave it to me for safekeeping years ago, in case anything happened to her. She knew she had enemies."</p>

<p>Elena took the chip, her hand trembling slightly. "Why did she give it to you?"</p>

<p>"Because I was the only person she trusted. And because she knew that one day, someone would need to finish what she started."</p>

<p>Elena pocketed the chip. "Thank you, Clara."</p>

<p>"Don't thank me yet. The people who did this are dangerous. They have resources, connections, and a willingness to kill. And they're already inside our walls."</p>

<p>"I know."</p>

<p>"Then be careful, Elena. The truth is a weapon. But it can also be a death sentence."</p>

<p>Elena left the botanical gardens with the data chip burning a hole in her pocket. She had what she needed—a starting point, a direction, a name. But Clara's warning echoed in her mind. The enemy was already inside. And they would do anything to keep the truth buried.</p>

<p>As she walked through the corridors of Armstrong Central, she noticed something she had missed before. A shadow in a doorway. A figure that disappeared too quickly. The feeling of being watched.</p>

<p>The cover-up was not just about political survival. It was about protecting the people who had orchestrated the attack. And those people were closer than she had ever imagined.</p>

