<h1 class="chapter-title">7. Evidence</h1>

<p class="first-para">Elena worked through the night, her eyes glued to the data chip's contents. Patricia Okonkwo's investigation files were meticulous, a masterclass in forensic accounting and corporate espionage. Names, dates, transaction records, encrypted communications—all laid out in a logical chain that left no room for doubt. Dorado Holdings, a consortium of twelve energy corporations, had spent seven years and four billion dollars attempting to secure exclusive mining rights to the Moon's helium-3 deposits. They had bribed politicians, compromised regulators, and infiltrated the International Lunar Commission. And they had come within weeks of succeeding.</p>

<p>But Patricia had stopped them. She had traced the bribes, exposed the corruption, and built a case that sent three executives to prison and disbanded the consortium's leadership. Dorado had been crushed—publicly, decisively, and permanently.</p>

<p>Or so everyone thought.</p>

<p>Elena found the smoking gun in a series of encrypted messages recovered from a Dorado server in Zurich. The messages were dated six months ago, long after the consortium was supposed to have been dissolved. They referenced a "Project Nemesis"—a contingency plan for revenge against the lunar colony. The messages named a contact inside Armstrong Central, a person identified only by the code name "Cerberus."</p>

<p>Elena stared at the screen, her heart pounding. Cerberus. The three-headed dog that guarded the gates of Hell. A code name that suggested someone with multiple roles, multiple loyalties, multiple faces. Someone embedded deep in the colony's structure.</p>

<p>She cross-referenced the timeline with the colony's personnel records. Six months ago, there had been a crew rotation. Twelve new colonists had arrived from Earth. Among them were engineers, scientists, and support staff. Any one of them could be Cerberus.</p>

<p>But the messages suggested that Cerberus was already in place before the rotation. Someone who had been on the Moon for years, building trust, waiting for the right moment to strike.</p>

<p>Elena's thoughts turned to the leadership council. Chen, Okonkwo, Volkov—all of them had been on the Moon long enough to be compromised. But the evidence pointed to someone with access to the kitchen, the food supply, and the security systems. Someone who could delete footage without raising suspicion. Someone who knew the colony well enough to plan the attack with surgical precision.</p>

<p>She pulled up the personnel files of everyone with kitchen access. Eight names. All of them had been vetted by Earth security before deployment. All of them had clean records. But one name caught her attention: Diego Reyes, the sous-chef that Kowalski had interviewed. According to his file, he had been a culinary specialist on Earth before joining the colony. But his previous employer was a company called Gastronomix, which—according to Patricia's files—was a subsidiary of one of the Dorado consortium members.</p>

<p>The connection was thin but tantalizing. Elena flagged the file and continued digging.</p>

<p>By dawn, she had assembled a timeline: six months ago, the Dorado messages began referencing Project Nemesis. Four months ago, Diego Reyes applied for a position in the colony's kitchen staff under a reference from Gastronomix. Three months ago, his application was approved. Two months ago, he arrived on the Moon. One week ago, the anniversary celebration was planned. And yesterday, people started dying.</p>

<p>The pieces fit together with terrible precision. But there were gaps—questions that refused to yield answers. How had Reyes obtained the nanites? How had he introduced them into the food supply without being detected? And who else in the colony was working with him?</p>

<p>Elena's comm unit buzzed. "Dr. Vasquez, this is Kowalski. I've been reviewing the security footage logs more carefully. The deletion was not done from the kitchen terminal. It was done remotely, from a terminal in the command center."</p>

<p>"The command center? Who has access?"</p>

<p>"Only the governor, his chief of staff, and the head of security."</p>

<p>The implications hit Elena like a physical blow. "Kowalski, I need you to secure the data chip I gave you and meet me in Med-Bay. We need to talk in person."</p>

<p>"What's going on?"</p>

<p>"I think I know who Cerberus is. And I think I know why the governor is so determined to cover this up."</p>

<p>The line went silent. Then Kowalski's voice came back, hard and cold. "On my way."</p>

<p>Elena closed the files and stood, her legs unsteady from hours of sitting. She had the evidence she needed to prove the attack was orchestrated by Dorado. She had the thread that led to Diego Reyes. But the missing footage from the command center pointed to someone much higher in the chain. Someone who had the authority and the access to orchestrate a cover-up from the inside.</p>

<p>The question was whether that someone was acting alone, or whether they were working for the same people who had sent the nanites.</p>

<p>And the more pressing question: who could she trust?</p>

<!-- CHAPTER 8 -->

<h1 class="chapter-title">8. Accusation</h1>

<p class="first-para">Elena called an emergency meeting of the entire colony leadership. She did not ask permission. She stood in the center of the grand hall—the same hall where the anniversary celebration had been held—and announced over the public address system that all senior personnel were required to assemble immediately. The quarantine protocols made it difficult, but she invoked medical authority, a provision that overrode standard procedure during a health emergency.</p>

<p>By 09:00 hours, the leadership council had gathered, along with representatives from every department. The hall was filled with anxious faces, people who had been confined to their modules for two days, hungry for information and terrified of the unknown.</p>

<p>Governor Chen arrived last, his face a mask of controlled anger. "Dr. Vasquez, this is highly irregular. You do not have the authority to convene the entire colony—"</p>

<p>"I have medical authority under Section 7 of the Colony Charter," Elena interrupted. "And I am exercising it."</p>

<p>The crowd murmured. Chen's jaw tightened, but he said nothing.</p>

<p>Elena stepped onto the raised platform and faced the assembled colonists. "For the past two days, you have been told that a routine illness is being managed by medical staff. That is not true. You are the victims of a deliberate biological attack, engineered by a corporate consortium called Dorado Holdings, which seeks to destroy this colony and seize its resources."</p>

<p>The murmuring exploded into chaos. Voices rose in alarm, in disbelief, in anger. Chen stepped forward, his face red. "Dr. Vasquez, you are spreading dangerous and unsubstantiated rumors. I order you to stop this immediately."</p>

<p>"I have evidence, Governor. Evidence that you have been actively suppressing. Evidence that traces the attack to a mole inside this colony, code-named Cerberus, who orchestrated the delivery of engineered nanites through the anniversary food supply."</p>

<p>The crowd fell silent. All eyes turned to Chen.</p>

<p>"I have reviewed the security footage," Elena continued. "Seventeen minutes were deleted from the kitchen cameras on the morning of the celebration. The deletion was performed from a terminal in the command center. A terminal accessible only to you, the governor; your chief of staff, Patricia Okonkwo; and the head of security, Dmitri Volkov."</p>

<p>She turned to face each of them in turn. "One of you is Cerberus. One of you is responsible for the deaths of twelve colonists, with more dying every hour. And I will not stop until I find out which one."</p>

<p>Patricia Okonkwo's face was pale, but her voice was steady. "Dr. Vasquez, I understand your frustration. But these are serious accusations. You need to present your evidence to a proper investigative body, not to the entire colony in a public spectacle."</p>

<p>"The investigative body is compromised. The evidence was suppressed. The only way to ensure accountability is to bring it to the people who have the most to lose."</p>

<p>Dmitri Volkov stepped forward, his massive frame radiating menace. "You are accusing the governor's office of orchestrating a biological attack on the colony. Do you understand what you're saying?"</p>

<p>"I am saying that the evidence points to someone with command-center access. I am saying that the cover-up is as damning as the attack itself. And I am saying that I will not be silenced by threats or intimidation."</p>

<p>Chen raised his hands, signaling for calm. "Dr. Vasquez has raised serious questions. We will investigate them thoroughly. But in the meantime, we must maintain order. I am appointing a independent review panel to examine the evidence—"</p>

<p>"No," Elena said. "You don't get to control the investigation. Not when you are one of the suspects."</p>

<p>The crowd erupted again. Voices shouted from all sides. Some called for Elena to be arrested. Others demanded answers from Chen. A few stood in silent shock, unable to process the magnitude of the revelation.</p>

<p>In the chaos, Elena caught Patricia Okonkwo's eye. There was something in her gaze—not anger, not denial, but fear. Patricia was afraid. But of what? Of being exposed? Or of something else entirely?</p>

<p>The meeting dissolved into arguments and recriminations. Elena stepped down from the platform and was immediately surrounded by colonists demanding answers, demanding proof, demanding action. She answered as best she could, but the truth was a weapon that cut both ways. She had exposed the cover-up, but she had also fractured the colony's trust in its leadership. And without trust, the quarantine would be impossible to maintain.</p>

<p>Raj pushed through the crowd and grabbed her arm. "We need to get you out of here. Now."</p>

<p>"I'm not leaving—"</p>

<p>"You've done what you needed to do. Now you need to stay alive long enough to see it through. Volkov's people are already moving. They're going to detain you."</p>

<p>Elena looked past Raj's shoulder and saw two security officers pushing through the crowd, their expressions grim. She made a decision. "The botanical gardens. Clara Morrison. She has the rest of the evidence."</p>

<p>Raj nodded and pulled her toward a side exit, the crowd parting around them. They slipped through a service corridor and into the maintenance tunnels that ran beneath the main habitat rings. The tunnels were dark, lit only by emergency strips, but Raj knew them well—he had helped design the hydroponics systems that ran through them.</p>

<p>"Where are we going?" Elena asked.</p>

<p>"Somewhere safe. I have a friend in Maintenance who owes me a favor."</p>

<p>They moved quickly through the tunnels, the sounds of the chaos above fading behind them. Elena's mind raced. She had thrown a grenade into the colony's political structure. Now she had to survive the shrapnel.</p>

