<h1 class="chapter-title">9. The Mole</h1>

<p class="first-para">The maintenance tunnels wound through the bones of Armstrong Central, a labyrinth of conduits, pipes, and access hatches that few colonists ever saw. Raj led Elena to a small workshop tucked behind a water reclamation unit—a cramped space filled with tools and spare parts, lit by a single flickering bulb. A man sat at a workbench, his back to them, repairing a circuit board with steady hands.</p>

<p>"Thomas," Raj said. "I need a favor."</p>

<p>The man turned. It was Thomas Park, the life-support manager—one of the five people with access to the command center. Elena tensed, but Raj placed a calming hand on her arm.</p>

<p>"He's not the mole," Raj said. "He's been helping me analyze the nanite delivery system. He's the one who identified the Gastronomix connection in the supply chain."</p>

<p>Thomas Park was a quiet man in his fifties, with graying hair and the calloused hands of someone who worked with machinery daily. He nodded at Elena without surprise. "I've been expecting you, Dr. Vasquez. Sit down."</p>

<p>Elena remained standing. "If you're not the mole, then who is?"</p>

<p>"I don't know for certain. But I've been tracking anomalous system access for the past three months. Someone has been using the command center terminals outside of normal hours. Late at night, when the governor and his staff are asleep."</p>

<p>"Who?"</p>

<p>"That's the problem. The access logs show the governor's credentials were used. But I've verified—Marcus Chen was in his quarters during every one of those late-night sessions. His biometrics didn't match. Someone cloned his access codes."</p>

<p>"Cloned codes. That means someone in IT."</p>

<p>"Or someone with physical access to the governor's terminal."</p>

<p>Elena's mind raced through the possibilities. Chen's terminal was in his private office, accessible only through his quarters. The security was layered—biometric, passcode, physical key. Cloning all three would require inside knowledge and repeated access.</p>

<p>"Who has been in Chen's quarters recently?" Elena asked.</p>

<p>Thomas pulled up a log on his tablet. "Patricia Okonkwo, for daily briefings. Dmitri Volkov, for security consultations. And a cleaning staff member named Anita Kowalski. No relation to James, before you ask."</p>

<p>Elena frowned. "Anita Kowalski. What do we know about her?"</p>

<p>"She's been with the colony for two years. Clean record. No disciplinary issues. But she transferred from a different posting—she was originally assigned to a Earth-side facility owned by... Gastronomix."</p>

<p>The name again. Gastronomix, the Dorado subsidiary. The thread that kept appearing every time they dug deeper.</p>

<p>"Where is Anita now?" Elena asked.</p>

<p>"She reported for duty this morning, then left early, citing illness. She's in her quarters in Habitat D."</p>

<p>Elena turned to Raj. "We need to detain her before Volkov's people find us."</p>

<p>"We can't just detain a colonist without cause—"</p>

<p>"She's the mole, Raj. She had access to the governor's credentials. She worked for a Dorado subsidiary. She disappeared right after I made my accusation public. That's enough cause."</p>

<p>Thomas stood, grabbing a tool belt. "I know a way to Habitat D that avoids the main corridors. Follow me."</p>

<p>They moved through the tunnels again, navigating by memory and instinct. The colony was in chaos above them, but the underground was silent, the only sounds the hum of machinery and the soft padding of their footsteps. Elena's heart pounded in her chest. They were getting close. She could feel it.</p>

<p>Thomas stopped at a service hatch and peered through a small grille. "Habitat D is just beyond this wall. Her module is third from the left."</p>

<p>Elena pressed her eye to the grille. The corridor was empty, the quarantine keeping everyone inside their modules. "We go in fast and quiet. Thomas, cover the door. Raj, with me."</p>

<p>They opened the hatch and stepped into the corridor. The lights were dimmed for the night cycle, casting long shadows. They reached Anita's module door, and Elena pressed the intercom.</p>

<p>"Anita Kowalski. This is Dr. Elena Vasquez. I need to speak with you."</p>

<p>Silence. Then a voice, thin and frightened. "Go away. I don't know anything."</p>

<p>"Anita, I'm not here to hurt you. I'm here to protect you. The people you're working for—they will discard you the moment you're no longer useful. Help me, and I can keep you safe."</p>

<p>More silence. Then the door slid open.</p>

<p>Anita Kowalski stood in the doorway, a small woman with nervous eyes and a trembling hands. Behind her, Elena could see a data terminal displaying encrypted messages. The screen was active, the cursor blinking on a half-typed transmission.</p>

<p>"I didn't know," Anita whispered. "When I took the job, I didn't know what they were planning. They told me it was industrial espionage. Stealing mining data. I didn't know about the nanites. I didn't know people would die."</p>

<p>"Who recruited you?"</p>

<p>"A man. He called himself Mr. Dorado. He said he worked for a private intelligence firm. He paid me to clone the governor's access codes and plant a device in the kitchen ventilation system. That's all I did. I swear."</p>

<p>"The device in the kitchen—what did it look like?"</p>

<p>"Small. Metallic. Shaped like a coin. I attached it to the underside of the prep table where they mixed the anniversary cake batter."</p>

<p>Elena's blood ran cold. The delivery mechanism wasn't in the food itself—it was in the air. The nanites had been released into the kitchen environment, settling on surfaces, mixing with ingredients, being inhaled by the staff. The cake was just one vector. Everyone who had been in the kitchen during preparation had been exposed.</p>

<p>"Who else is involved?" Elena demanded. "Who's Cerberus?"</p>

<p>Anita's face went pale. "I don't know. I never met them. Mr. Dorado said there was someone inside the leadership, someone who would protect the operation. But he never told me who."</p>

<p>"Think, Anita. Did he ever say anything that might identify them? A name, a detail, anything?"</p>

<p>Anita's brow furrowed. "Once. He said Cerberus was close to the governor. Had been for years. And that Cerberus had more to lose than anyone."</p>

<p>Close to the governor. More to lose than anyone. The description could fit Patricia Okonkwo or Dmitri Volkov. But there was a third possibility—someone Elena had not considered. Someone whose access and proximity made them the perfect spy.</p>

<p>The governor's own spouse.</p>

