<h1 class="chapter-title">13. Betrayal</h1>

<p class="first-para">The shuttle vanished into the darkness, its engine trail fading like a dying star. Elena stood at the observation window, the sidearm hanging uselessly at her side. Patricia Okonkwo was gone. The mastermind of the attack had escaped, and Elena had let her go.</p>

<p>But the battle was not over. The colony was still in chaos. The patients still needed treatment. And Elena still had to confront the person who had enabled everything: Dmitri Volkov.</p>

<p>She found him in the security command center, alone, staring at a bank of monitors showing the empty corridors of the colony. He did not turn as she entered. "You let her go."</p>

<p>"I couldn't stop her."</p>

<p>"You could have shot her."</p>

<p>"I'm a doctor. I save lives, not take them."</p>

<p>Volkov finally turned. His face was tired, the hard lines softened by something that looked like regret. "She was going to take me with her. Did you know that? At the last minute, she told me I was too much baggage. That I had to stay behind and face the consequences."</p>

<p>"She used you."</p>

<p>"I know." He sat down heavily, his massive frame collapsing into the chair. "I've known for years. But the money was good, and I told myself it was just security consulting. I didn't ask questions."</p>

