<p>Or so everyone thought. Elena found the smoking gun in a series of encrypted messages recovered from a Dorado server in Zurich. The messages were dated six months ago, long after the consortium was supposed to have been dissolved. They referenced a "Project Nemesis"—a contingency plan for revenge against the lunar colony. The messages named a contact inside Armstrong Central, a person identified only by the code name "Cerberus." Elena stared at the screen, her heart pounding. Cerberus. The three-headed dog that guarded the gates of Hell. A code name that suggested someone with multiple roles, multiple loyalties, multiple faces. Someone embedded deep in the colony's structure.</p>

<p>She cross-referenced the timeline with the colony's personnel records. Six months ago, there had been a crew rotation. Twelve new colonists had arrived from Earth. Among them were engineers, scientists, and support staff. Any one of them could be Cerberus. But the messages suggested that Cerberus was already in place before the rotation. Someone who had been on the Moon for years, building trust, waiting for the right moment to strike. Elena's thoughts turned to the leadership council. Chen, Okonkwo, Volkov—all of them had been on the Moon long enough to be compromised. But the evidence pointed to someone with access to the kitchen, the food supply, and the security systems. Someone who could delete footage without raising suspicion. Someone who knew the colony well enough to plan the attack with surgical precision.</p>

<p>She pulled up the personnel files of everyone with kitchen access. Eight names. All of them had been vetted by Earth security before deployment. All of them had clean records. But one name caught her attention: Diego Reyes, the sous-chef that Kowalski had interviewed. According to his file, he had been a culinary specialist on Earth before joining the colony. But his previous employer was a company called Gastronomix, which—according to Patricia's files—was a subsidiary of one of the Dorado consortium members. The connection was thin but tantalizing. Elena flagged the file and continued digging. By dawn, she had assembled a timeline: six months ago, the Dorado messages began referencing Project Nemesis. Four months ago, Diego Reyes applied for a position in the colony's kitchen staff under a reference from Gastronomix. Three months ago, his application was approved. Two months ago, he arrived on the Moon. One week ago, the anniversary celebration was planned. And yesterday, people started dying. The pieces fit together with terrible precision. But there were gaps—questions that refused to yield answers. How had Reyes obtained the nanites? How had he introduced them into the food supply without being detected? And who else in the colony was working with him?</p>

<p>Elena's comm unit buzzed. "Dr. Vasquez, this is Kowalski. I've been reviewing the security footage logs more carefully. The deletion was not done from the kitchen terminal. It was done remotely, from a terminal in the command center." "The command center? Who has access?" "Only the governor, his chief of staff, and the head of security." The implications hit Elena like a physical blow. "Kowalski, I need you to secure the data chip I gave you and meet me in Med-Bay. We need to talk in person." "What's going on?" "I think I know who Cerberus is. And I think I know why the governor is so determined to cover this up."</p>

<p>The line went silent. Then Kowalski's voice came back, hard and cold. "On my way." Elena closed the files and stood, her legs unsteady from hours of sitting. She had the evidence she needed to prove the attack was orchestrated by Dorado. She had the thread that led to Diego Reyes. But the missing footage from the command center pointed to someone much higher in the chain. Someone who had the authority and the access to orchestrate a cover-up from the inside. The question was whether that someone was acting alone, or whether they were working for the same people who had sent the nanites. And the more pressing question: who could she trust? She looked at the data chip in her hand, then at the door, then back at the chip. The evidence was damning. But evidence was only as powerful as the people willing to act on it. And right now, Elena was not sure she had anyone she could trust except herself.</p>

