<h1 class="chapter-title">10. Confrontation</h1>

<p class="first-para">Marcus Chen's wife, Dr. Li Na Chen, was a botanist who had been part of the original settlement team. She was quiet, respected, and largely invisible in the colony's political life—by design, Elena realized now. The perfect cover. The person closest to the governor, with unimpeachable access to his quarters, his terminal, his every private conversation. She had more to lose than anyone because she had everything: position, respect, and the ear of the most powerful person on the Moon. Elena left Anita in Thomas's custody and made her way to the Chen family quarters, located in the executive wing of Habitat A. The corridors were deserted, the colony in lockdown. She moved with purpose, her footsteps echoing in the silence. The door to the Chen quarters was guarded by a security officer who recognized her and stepped aside. "Dr. Vasquez. The governor is not receiving visitors." "I'm not here to see the governor. I'm here to see his wife." The officer hesitated, then nodded. "She's in the private garden. Down the hall, second door on the left."</p>

<p>Elena found the garden—a small room filled with potted plants and artificial light, a sanctuary of green in the gray metal world of the colony. Li Na Chen was tending to a flowering orchid, her movements gentle and precise. She looked up as Elena entered, her expression serene. "Dr. Vasquez. I was wondering when you would come." "You knew I would." "I knew someone would. The truth has a way of finding its way to the surface." She set down her watering can and turned to face Elena fully. "How much do you know?" "Enough. You're Cerberus. You've been working for Dorado since before the colony was founded. You married Marcus to get close to power. And you've been feeding information to your handlers for years, waiting for the moment to strike."</p>

<p>Li Na smiled, a cold expression that never reached her eyes. "You're partially correct. I am Cerberus. But I didn't marry Marcus for access. I married him because I loved him. The intelligence work was... secondary." "You loved him enough to help destroy everything he built?" "I tried to protect him. When Dorado threatened to expose me, I negotiated. I told them I would facilitate the attack, but I demanded that Marcus be kept safe. I demanded that no harm come to him directly." "But harm came to everyone else. Twelve people are in comas. A child forgot her mother's face." Li Na's composure cracked, just slightly. "I didn't know about the nanites. Not until it was too late. Mr. Dorado told me it was a sedative—a temporary incapacitant that would force the colony to negotiate. I didn't know it was designed to destroy minds."</p>

<p>"You expect me to believe that?" "I don't expect you to believe anything. But it's the truth." She paused, her voice dropping. "I've been trying to stop it. I've been feeding information to Patricia—she knows about the Dorado connection, about the mole, about everything. She's been building a case against them from the inside." "Patricia? But she's the one who prosecuted Dorado. Why would she—" "Because she's also Cerberus." The words hit Elena like a physical blow. "What?" "Patricia Okonkwo was the first recruit. She was the one who put me in touch with Dorado. She prosecuted the case to establish her cover, to make herself untouchable. And then she worked her way into Marcus's inner circle to protect the operation."</p>

<p>Elena's mind reeled. Patricia Okonkwo—the woman who had prosecuted Dorado, the woman who had built the case against them—was one of them. The perfect double agent. The betrayal was so deep, so layered, that Elena could barely process it. "Why are you telling me this?" "Because I'm tired. Because I've been living a lie for fifteen years, and I want it to end. Because if this colony falls, everything I've sacrificed will have been for nothing." Li Na's eyes glistened. "I love Marcus. I love this colony. And I have been complicit in its destruction. I need to make it right." "How?" "Patricia is planning to flee. She has a private shuttle scheduled to depart in six hours, bound for a Dorado facility in Earth orbit. If she reaches that shuttle, she disappears forever, and all the evidence goes with her." "Then we stop her."</p>

<p>Li Na shook her head. "You can't. She has the entire security force under her control. Volkov is her partner—he's been in on it from the beginning. If you try to detain her, she'll have you killed." "Then what do you suggest?" "There's one person she can't control. One person whose authority supersedes hers." "Marcus." "Marcus." Li Na's voice cracked. "I have to tell him everything. About me, about Patricia, about the attack. And then I have to let him decide what to do with me." Elena studied the woman before her, searching for deception. But she saw only exhaustion and regret. "Do it. Now. I'll be with you."</p>

<p>Li Na nodded and walked toward the door that connected the garden to the governor's private study. Elena followed, her hand resting on the medical scanner she carried—a poor weapon, but the only one she had. The study door opened, and Marcus Chen looked up from his desk, surprise and anger warring on his face. "Elena. Li Na. What is the meaning of—" Li Na walked to him, took his hands, and began to speak. The words came out in a rush, a confession years in the making. And as Elena watched the governor's face transform from anger to shock to devastation, she knew that nothing would ever be the same. The betrayal was complete, the conspiracy laid bare. And now they had to survive long enough to do something about it.</p>

