<h1 class="chapter-title">12. The Truth Serum</h1>

<p class="first-para">The chaos was immediate and total. Patricia Okonkwo's voice echoed through the colony's intercom system, countermanding Chen's authority. "This is Chief of Staff Okonkwo. Governor Chen has been compromised. He is suffering from the same neurological condition affecting our colonists. I am assuming command under emergency protocols. All security personnel report to my location immediately." Half the colony didn't know whom to believe. The other half had already taken sides. In the communications center, Chen turned to Elena, his face pale. "She's staging a coup." "She's accelerating her timeline. She knows Earth is coming. She needs to be gone before they arrive." "We can't let her reach that shuttle." "Then we don't. We lock down the shuttle bay."</p>

<p>Chen nodded and began issuing orders through his personal comm unit, bypassing the compromised security network. He had enough loyalists scattered through the colony to make a difference—engineers, medical staff, old friends who had known him for years. They would not all follow Patricia's orders. Elena's comm buzzed. It was Yuki Tanaka. "Dr. Vasquez, I've completed the electromagnetic device. I need a test subject." "Use the comatose patients. Start with the first one. Report results immediately." "Understood." Elena turned back to Chen. "If Yuki's device works, we can start treating patients. That changes everything." "How?" "Treatment gives us evidence that the attack was deliberate. It proves the nanites were engineered. It validates everything we've been saying."</p>

<p>Minutes stretched into hours. The colony remained in a state of suspended animation, two factions staring at each other across an invisible line. Patricia had control of the security forces and the shuttle bay. Chen had control of the communications center, the medical wing, and the loyalty of most of the civilian population. Elena moved between Med-Bay and the command center, coordinating the response, checking on the patients, monitoring Yuki's progress. The patients were deteriorating. Three more had slipped into comas. Mei Lin, the eight-year-old girl, was barely responsive. Her mother had been allowed to sit with her, and the sight of the woman holding her daughter's hand, whispering words the girl could no longer understand, nearly broke Elena's resolve. She pushed through. She had to.</p>

<p>Then Yuki's call came. "It works. The first patient is showing signs of neurological recovery. Nanite activity has ceased. Neural pathways are beginning to regenerate." Elena felt a surge of hope so powerful it nearly brought her to her knees. "How long until you can treat everyone?" "I need to build more devices. With the resources available, I can treat ten patients per hour. All forty-seven affected colonists can be treated within five hours." "Start immediately. Priority goes to the most severe cases." "Understood." Elena turned to Chen, her voice stronger than it had been in days. "We have a treatment. We can save everyone."</p>

<p>Chen's eyes glistened. "Then we have a future. Patricia can run, but she can't hide from what we've built." "She's still trying to escape. We need to stop her." "I have people at the shuttle bay. They've reported that the shuttle is prepped and fueled. Patricia is on her way there now, accompanied by Volkov and a security detail." "How many in the detail?" "Six. Armed." Elena looked at the medical scanner in her hand—a diagnostic tool, not a weapon. "I need something to fight with." Chen opened a locked drawer in his desk and pulled out a compact sidearm. "Standard issue for emergency situations. I never thought I'd have to use it on one of my own people."</p>

<p>Elena took the weapon, checking the charge. "You won't have to. I'll handle it." "Elena, you're a doctor—" "I'm also a colonist. And I'm not going to let Patricia Okonkwo destroy everything we've built." She left the communications center and moved through the corridors toward the shuttle bay. The colony was eerily quiet, the usual hum of activity replaced by tense silence. Doors were sealed. Lights were dimmed. Everyone was waiting. She reached the access corridor to the shuttle bay and found it blocked by two security officers. They saw her and raised their weapons. "Dr. Vasquez. You're under arrest by order of Acting Governor Okonkwo." Elena raised her hands, the sidearm hidden behind her back. "I'm not here to fight. I'm here to talk. Let me speak to Patricia." "She's not taking visitors." "Tell her I have information about the treatment. Tell her I know how to cure the nanite infection." The officers exchanged glances. One of them tapped his comm unit and murmured into it. A moment later, the door behind them slid open, and Patricia Okonkwo appeared, dressed in a flight suit, a bag over her shoulder.</p>

<p>"Dr. Vasquez. You have something to tell me?" "I have a treatment for the nanites. Electromagnetic disruption. It neutralizes them within minutes." Patricia's eyes narrowed. "Why are you telling me this?" "Because I want you to know that you failed. The people you tried to destroy are going to live. The colony is going to survive. And you're going to spend the rest of your life running from the consequences of what you've done." Patricia's face hardened. "You think this is over. It's not. Dorado has resources you can't imagine. They will try again. And next time, they won't make the same mistakes." "Next time, we'll be ready." Patricia turned and walked toward the shuttle. Elena raised the sidearm. "Stop. I don't want to shoot you, but I will." Patricia paused, looking back over her shoulder. "You won't. You're a healer, not a killer." She was right. Elena's hand trembled. She could not pull the trigger. Patricia boarded the shuttle. The engines hummed to life. And Elena watched as the woman who had orchestrated the destruction of her colony lifted off from the lunar surface, carrying her secrets into the black void of space. The sidearm hung uselessly at her side. She had stopped the nanites. She had saved the patients. But she had let the mastermind escape. It was a victory, but not a complete one.</p>

<!-- CHAPTER 13 -->

<h1 class="chapter-title">13. Betrayal</h1>

<p class="first-para">The shuttle vanished into the darkness, its engine trail fading like a dying star. Elena stood at the observation window, the sidearm hanging uselessly at her side. Patricia Okonkwo was gone. The mastermind of the attack had escaped, and Elena had let her go. But the battle was not over. The colony was still in chaos. The patients still needed treatment. And Elena still had to confront the person who had enabled everything: Dmitri Volkov. She found him in the security command center, alone, staring at a bank of monitors showing the empty corridors of the colony. He did not turn as she entered. "You let her go." "I couldn't stop her." "You could have shot her." "I'm a doctor. I save lives, not take them." Volkov finally turned. His face was tired, the hard lines softened by something that looked like regret. "She was going to take me with her. Did you know that? At the last minute, she told me I was too much baggage. That I had to stay behind and face the consequences." "She used you." "I know." He sat down heavily, his massive frame collapsing into the chair. "I've known for years. But the money was good, and I told myself it was just security consulting. I didn't ask questions." "You enabled a biological attack on your own people." "I didn't know about the nanites. Not until people started getting sick. By then, I was in too deep. Patricia had evidence of my involvement. She would have destroyed me." "She destroyed you anyway. You're going to prison for the rest of your life." Volkov nodded slowly. "I know. And I'll accept that. But before I do, I want to help. I want to make things right." "How?" "I have records. Everything Patricia did, everything she communicated, every payment she received. I kept copies, hidden in a secure location. If I give them to you, you can trace the entire conspiracy back to Earth. You can bring down Dorado for good."</p>

<p>Elena studied him, searching for deception. But she saw only a broken man, stripped of his power and his illusions. "Where are the records?" "In my personal safe. The access code is my mother's birthday. I'll give it to you now." He typed the code into his terminal and a data file appeared—thousands of pages of encrypted communications, financial transactions, and operational plans. Elena transferred the file to her own device, her hands moving quickly. "Thank you," she said. "Don't thank me. I'm still responsible for what happened. I'll face trial, and I'll accept whatever sentence comes." He paused. "But there's something else you should know. Patricia wasn't the only mole. There's someone else, someone higher up, who was coordinating with Dorado from Earth. Someone who has been protecting this operation for years." Elena's blood ran cold. "Who?" "I don't know their name. But Patricia mentioned them once. She called them 'the Architect.' They're a senior executive in the Dorado consortium, and they orchestrated the entire plan. Patricia was just the field agent." "How do I find the Architect?" "You can't. But Earth's investigation team will be here soon. Give them the records. Let them follow the trail."</p>

<p>Elena nodded, pocketing the data. She left Volkov in the command center, alone with his guilt and his monitors. Outside, the colony was slowly coming back to life. Yuki's treatment was working. Patients were waking up. The crisis was passing. But the betrayal lingered, a poison that would take years to fully purge. Li Na Chen had betrayed her husband. Patricia Okonkwo had betrayed her colleagues. Dmitri Volkov had betrayed his oath. And somewhere on Earth, the Architect was already planning the next phase of the assault. Elena walked through the corridors of Armstrong Central, past the medical teams tending to recovering patients, past the engineers restoring normal operations, past the families reunited after days of separation. The colony was healing, but the scars would remain. Trust, once broken, was the hardest thing to rebuild.</p>

<p>She found Raj Patel in Med-Bay, his face haggard but his eyes bright. "We've treated twenty-three patients so far. All responding well. No permanent neurological damage." "That's good news." "It's the best news we've had in days." He paused, studying her face. "You look like you've been through a war." "I have. And the war isn't over. There's someone on Earth, someone called the Architect, who orchestrated all of this. They're still out there." "Then we'll find them. Together." Elena allowed herself a small smile. "Together." The colony had survived the attack. But the battle for justice—for accountability, for trust, for the future—had only just begun. Elena looked at the data chip in her hand, then at the monitors showing recovering patients, then at Raj's tired but determined face. She had lost Patricia. But she had gained something more valuable: the truth. And the truth, wielded properly, was more powerful than any weapon.</p>

